  oversized values, and a documented convention that auth state never goes through this API). I'll make that explicit
  rather than leave it implied.

  Next question — this is the fork that's expensive to get wrong, so here's the comparison before the options.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a preference belongs to [3mthis browser[0m or to [3mthis user[0m. That decides whether there's a network
  round-trip in the read path, whether [38;5;153mget()[39m can be synchronous, and whether preferences follow someone to a second
  device.

  - [1mBrowser [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, client-only[0m — buys you a working feature today with zero dependencies and a synchronous [38;5;153mget()[39m.
    Costs: preferences are per-device and vanish when storage is cleared. Rules out cross-device sync until you add a
    backend.
  - [1mServer-backed, per user account[0m — buys cross-device persistence and survives a cleared browser. Costs: this repo has
    no backend at all ([38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m), so you'd be building an API, a store, and
    auth-scoped reads first. Also forces every read to be async.
  - [1mLocal now, sync-ready boundary[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m today, but the module's interface is shaped so a remote backend can
    slot in later. Costs: a little indirection you don't use yet, and the async question doesn't actually go away — you
    either pay async now or change the signature later.

  [1mMy recommendation: client-only [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m There's no backend to hang server-side preferences on, and building one
  is a much larger project than the request implies. The important part of "sync-ready" comes free just from putting
  this behind a small module instead of scattering [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m calls through [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — you get the swap point without
  designing for a backend that may never exist. If cross-device sync is actually a requirement, say so now, because that
  reverses the recommendation and makes this a substantially bigger piece of work.
